Problem 1 – Missing error handling  
• What is wrong: If the network request fails or the server returns a non-2xx status, res.json() will throw and the caller gets an unhandled rejection.  
• Fix: Wrap the fetch in try/​catch and check res.ok.

```
async function fetchPrice(productId) {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/price/${encodeURIComponent(productId)}`);
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
    return await res.json();
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Price request failed', err);
    throw err;               // or return a default / cached value
  }
}
```

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Problem 2 – No validation / encoding of productId  
• What is wrong: productId is concatenated directly into the URL. “/api/price/../../etc/passwd”-style path traversal or CRLF injection is possible if the server does naïve routing.  
• Fix: Encode it with encodeURIComponent (shown above) or pass it in the query string or body.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Problem 3 – Insecure storage in localStorage  
• What is wrong: localStorage is readable/​writable by any script that runs in the page. A single XSS bug anywhere in the site lets the attacker set an arbitrary price and/or read business-sensitive pricing data.  
• Improvements:
  – If you do not need the data across tabs or after reload, use sessionStorage or an in-memory cache instead.  
  – If you must persist, sign or hash the value so that tampering can be detected, or store the price only for display purposes, never for checkout calculations (re-obtain it from the server at the point of purchase).  
  – Make sure the site has a strong CSP and audit for XSS.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Problem 4 – Stale or unlimited cache  
• What is wrong: Once a price is stored it is kept forever; the user will never see price changes or promotions.  
• Fix: Store a timestamp/TTL together with the value and invalidate after e.g. 15 min:

```
const key = `price_${productId}`;
const now = Date.now();
const MAX_AGE = 15 * 60 * 1000; // 15 min

const cached = localStorage.getItem(key);
if (cached) {
  const { value, ts } = JSON.parse(cached);
  if (now - ts < MAX_AGE) return value;
}
...
localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify({ value: price, ts: now }));
```

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Problem 5 – Wrong (or fragile) serialization  
• What is wrong: localStorage.setItem receives price which may be an object (e.g. `{ price: 9.99, currency: "USD" }`).  
 The code relies on implicit toString() which would store “[object Object]”.  
• Fix: Use JSON.stringify when writing, and JSON.parse when reading.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Problem 6 – parseFloat/​NaN  
• What is wrong: parseFloat silently returns NaN if the cached string is anything but a number. If the value is a JSON object it will just drop everything after the first “{” and still produce NaN.  
• Fix: Parse the JSON you stored, validate it, and convert only the numeric part, e.g.:

```
const cached = localStorage.getItem(key);
if (cached) {
  const { value } = JSON.parse(cached);
  if (typeof value === 'number' && !Number.isNaN(value)) return value;
}
```

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Problem 7 – Credentials / CSRF  
• What is wrong: fetch is called without the { credentials: 'include' } option, so if your API relies on cookies for auth you may inadvertently call an unauthenticated endpoint and leak different or internal prices.  
• Fix: Add the option if needed:

```
await fetch(url, { credentials: 'include' });
```

Also protect the endpoint itself with CSRF tokens or same-site cookies.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Problem 8 – Concurrent calls (optional)  
Two components calling getPrice(42) at the same time will both hit the network before either puts the result in localStorage.  
• Fix: add an in-flight promise cache or memoize the function.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Re-written function (summary)

```
const PRICE_TTL = 15 * 60 * 1000;   // 15 min
const inFlight = new Map();

export async function getPrice(productId) {
  const key = `price_${productId}`;
  const now = Date.now();

  // Memory-level “in-flight” cache to avoid duplicate requests
  if (inFlight.has(productId)) return inFlight.get(productId);

  // LocalStorage cache with TTL
  try {
    const cached = localStorage.getItem(key);
    if (cached) {
      const { value, ts } = JSON.parse(cached);
      if (now - ts < PRICE_TTL) return value;
    }
  } catch (e) {
    console.warn('Cache read failed', e);
  }

  const request = (async () => {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/price/${encodeURIComponent(productId)}`, {
      credentials: 'include',
      headers: { 'Accept': 'application/json' }
    });
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
    const data = await res.json();

    if (typeof data.price !== 'number') throw new Error('Invalid payload');

    try {
      localStorage.setItem(
        key,
        JSON.stringify({ value: data.price, ts: Date.now() })
      );
    } catch (e) {
      console.warn('Cache write failed', e);
    }
    return data.price;
  })();

  inFlight.set(productId, request);
  try {
    return await request;
  } finally {
    inFlight.delete(productId);
  }
}
```

This version:

• Encodes user input,  
• Handles network/JSON errors,  
• Uses credentials safely,  
• Serializes/deserializes correctly,  
• Detects stale data and allows refresh,  
• Mitigates XSS/localStorage tampering risk (as far as client code can), and  
• Prevents duplicate simultaneous requests.

Apply whichever of these fixes make sense for your threat model and performance requirements.
